{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QdeJSuYYDCxfHSGfZmTjQo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# pace-bot\n2\t\n3\tA coaching chat bot on Claude for a small running/cycling club. It is stateless\n4\tby design: each message rebuilds the system prompt (coaching rules + the athlete's\n5\tprofile, zones, goal and long-term facts from `data/athletes.json`), picks the tools\n6\tthe message might need (`save_fact` and `log_session` always, planning or race tools\n7\twhen keywords match) and runs a short tool-use loop. `save_fact` is how the bot\n8\tremembers durable things an athlete tells it; the next message sees the fact in\n9\tits system prompt. Every API call appends its `usage` object to `logs/usage.jsonl`.\n10\t\n11\tRun it locally with `COACH_ANTHROPIC_KEY=sk-ant-... python3 bot.py 42` and type\n12\tmessages on stdin (the Telegram long-polling transport, which needs\n13\t`COACH_TELEGRAM_TOKEN`, lives in a separate deploy repo and just calls `reply()`).\n14\tRequires Python 3.11+ and `pip install anthropic`. Prompt caching is on: the system\n15\tprompt carries a `cache_control` marker so the tool-loop rounds and follow-up\n16\tmessages inside the 5-minute window read it from cache.\n17\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a01de13-6568-48fb-a169-7762714394bb","uuid":"ef83400f-974e-4329-8fed-8834a1e55f76","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:43:52.724Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/h-IgFUak/README.md","content":"# pace-bot\n\nA coaching chat bot on Claude for a small running/cycling club. It is stateless\nby design: each message rebuilds the system prompt (coaching rules + the athlete's\nprofile, zones, goal and long-term facts from `data/athletes.json`), picks the tools\nthe message might need (`save_fact` and `log_session` always, planning or race tools\nwhen keywords match) and runs a short tool-use loop. `save_fact` is how the bot\nremembers durable things an athlete tells it; the next message sees the fact in\nits system prompt. Every API call appends its `usage` object to `logs/usage.jsonl`.\n\nRun it locally with `COACH_ANTHROPIC_KEY=sk-ant-... python3 bot.py 42` and type\nmessages on stdin (the Telegram long-polling transport, which needs\n`COACH_TELEGRAM_TOKEN`, lives in a separate deploy repo and just calls `reply()`).\nRequires Python 3.11+ and `pip install anthropic`. Prompt caching is on: the system\nprompt carries a `cache_control` marker so the tool-loop rounds and follow-up\nmessages inside the 5-minute window read it from cache.\n","numLines":17,"startLine":1,"totalLines":17}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QdeJSuYYDCxfHSGfZmTjQo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgENq2hNTffiDLCbgndVy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RHnG2WRjWbY2NTSU5KVust","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-IgFUak/notes/two-cache-control-segments-so-agent-memory-writes-dont-evict.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":706,"cache_read_input_tokens":21162,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":706},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a01de13-6568-48fb-a169-7762714394bb","uuid":"320e3718-a50c-4b5b-8ec5-68f1aac27a5a","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:43:52.928Z","request_id":"req_011CgENq2MXoAzsiuetgPPPp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01RHnG2WRjWbY2NTSU5KVust":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-IgFUak/notes/two-cache-control-segments-so-agent-memory-writes-dont-evict.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RHnG2WRjWbY2NTSU5KVust","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttitle: Two cache_control segments so agent memory writes don't evict the whole Claude system prompt\n3\tdate: 2026-04-24\n4\ttags: [claude, prompt-caching, llm-agents, token-costs, mcp, telegram-bot]\n5\ttype: success\n6\tstatus: n/a\n7\treproducible: true\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t# Two cache_control segments so agent memory writes don't evict the whole Claude system prompt\n11\t\n12\tA chat agent pays for its whole system prompt on every message — and on every\n13\titeration of every tool-use loop. Anthropic's prompt cache cuts cached reads\n14\tto ~10% of the input price, but it works as a **prefix hash**: change one\n15\tbyte, and everything from that byte to the end of the cached block is a miss.\n16\tThat interacts badly with agent memory — the moment your agent learns\n17\tsomething new about the user, it rewrites its own prompt.\n18\t\n19\tMy Telegram coach bot is stateless: each message is a fresh Claude call with\n20\tinstructions, the athlete's profile, zones, goal, long-term facts, and a set\n21\tof MCP tools; a tool-use loop re-sends all of it 3–5 times per answer.\n22\t\n23\tFirst win was unrelated to caching: sending all ~55 tool schemas with every\n24\t\"thanks!\" was silly, so tools are filtered by message keywords into groups\n25\t(a core set always included) — about 75% fewer tokens on simple messages.\n26\t\n27\tThen long-term memory arrived and broke caching. With a single cache marker\n28\tat the end of the system prompt, every `save_fact` call rewrote the facts\n29\tblock, changed the prefix hash, and evicted **everything** — including the\n30\tinstruction text that never changes.\n31\t\n32\tThe fix is embarrassingly small: two `cache_control: ephemeral` markers\n33\tinstead of one. Segment one is the static instructions (~780 tokens,\n34\tbyte-identical across users and days). Segment two is the per-user tail\n35\t(~240 tokens: date, profile, zones, goal, facts, language). A memory write\n36\tnow invalidates only the tail; the static prefix stays hot. Anthropic allows\n37\t4 markers per request — we use 2.\n38\t\n39\tThe non-obvious part was what we had to *refuse* to build:\n40\t\n41\t- **Top-N fact selection per message** (only inject facts relevant to the\n42\t  current question) — rejected. Any per-message selection makes the tail\n43\t  differ per message, so the tail cache never hits. Loading *all* facts every\n44\t  time is cheaper than loading fewer, differently each time.\n45\t- **Aggressive tool filtering** has the same tension: tools sit *above* the\n46\t  system prompt in the cached prefix, so a message that pulls in an extra\n47\t  tool group re-reads everything once. Liveable because the default group set\n48\t  covers most messages, and within one message's tool loop the list is\n49\t  constant — and the loop iterations are where the cache pays most.\n50\t\n51\tNet effect: within the 5-minute TTL, loop iterations and follow-up messages\n52\tread the prompt at ~10% price, and frequent memory writes re-cache ~240\n53\ttokens instead of the ~1000+ of the whole prompt.\n54\t\n55\t## Reproduce this\n56\t\n57\tPrerequisites: an app calling the Claude API directly with the\n58\t[Anthropic SDK](https://docs.claude.com/en/api/client-sdks) and using\n59\t[prompt caching](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching);\n60\ta system prompt with a distinguishable invariant part (instructions) and a\n61\tvolatile part (per-user / per-day data, agent memory). Works for any\n62\tprovider with prefix-based caching — the marker syntax below is\n63\tAnthropic-specific.\n64\t\n65\tYour agent will need from you: your current system-prompt assembly code, and\n66\ta list of which parts change per user, per day, or per memory write.\n67\t\n68\tSteps:\n69\t\n70\t1. Split `system` into an array of two text blocks, each with\n71\t   `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}`: block one = instructions,\n72\t   guaranteed byte-stable (no interpolated dates, names, or counters);\n73\t   block two = everything volatile (date, profile, memory), ordered\n74\t   rarest-changing first for readability — after the marker it's one hash\n75\t   anyway.\n76\t2. Keep everything *above* the system prompt in the request — the tools\n77\t   array most of all — byte-stable across calls; if you must vary tools per\n78\t   message, make the default selection cover the common case, and never\n79\t   reorder or reword tool schemas casually.\n80\t3. Resist per-message personalization inside cached blocks (top-N memory\n81\t   selection, \"relevant context only\") — selection defeats the cache;\n82\t   include-everything usually costs less.\n83\t4. Read `usage` in every response: `cache_creation_input_tokens` and\n84\t   `cache_read_input_tokens` tell you exactly what happened.\n85\t\n86\tVerify: send two messages within 5 minutes — the second must show\n87\t`cache_read_input_tokens` covering roughly the whole prompt. Then trigger a\n88\tmemory write and send a third message: `cache_read` must still cover the\n89\tstatic block, and `cache_creation` must be roughly the size of your volatile\n90\ttail only. If a memory write causes `cache_creation` the size of the full\n91\tprompt, your static block isn't byte-stable — hunt the interpolated value.\n92\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a01de13-6568-48fb-a169-7762714394bb","uuid":"c92ffe5c-9d7f-46f1-87de-5c6f11d941c8","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:43:52.942Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/h-IgFUak/notes/two-cache-control-segments-so-agent-memory-writes-dont-evict.md","content":"---\ntitle: Two cache_control segments so agent memory writes don't evict the whole Claude system prompt\ndate: 2026-04-24\ntags: [claude, prompt-caching, llm-agents, token-costs, mcp, telegram-bot]\ntype: success\nstatus: n/a\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# Two cache_control segments so agent memory writes don't evict the whole Claude system prompt\n\nA chat agent pays for its whole system prompt on every message — and on every\niteration of every tool-use loop. Anthropic's prompt cache cuts cached reads\nto ~10% of the input price, but it works as a **prefix hash**: change one\nbyte, and everything from that byte to the end of the cached block is a miss.\nThat interacts badly with agent memory — the moment your agent learns\nsomething new about the user, it rewrites its own prompt.\n\nMy Telegram coach bot is stateless: each message is a fresh Claude call with\ninstructions, the athlete's profile, zones, goal, long-term facts, and a set\nof MCP tools; a tool-use loop re-sends all of it 3–5 times per answer.\n\nFirst win was unrelated to caching: sending all ~55 tool schemas with every\n\"thanks!\" was silly, so tools are filtered by message keywords into groups\n(a core set always included) — about 75% fewer tokens on simple messages.\n\nThen long-term memory arrived and broke caching. With a single cache marker\nat the end of the system prompt, every `save_fact` call rewrote the facts\nblock, changed the prefix hash, and evicted **everything** — including the\ninstruction text that never changes.\n\nThe fix is embarrassingly small: two `cache_control: ephemeral` markers\ninstead of one. Segment one is the static instructions (~780 tokens,\nbyte-identical across users and days). Segment two is the per-user tail\n(~240 tokens: date, profile, zones, goal, facts, language). A memory write\nnow invalidates only the tail; the static prefix stays hot. Anthropic allows\n4 markers per request — we use 2.\n\nThe non-obvious part was what we had to *refuse* to build:\n\n- **Top-N fact selection per message** (only inject facts relevant to the\n  current question) — rejected. Any per-message selection makes the tail\n  differ per message, so the tail cache never hits. Loading *all* facts every\n  time is cheaper than loading fewer, differently each time.\n- **Aggressive tool filtering** has the same tension: tools sit *above* the\n  system prompt in the cached prefix, so a message that pulls in an extra\n  tool group re-reads everything once. Liveable because the default group set\n  covers most messages, and within one message's tool loop the list is\n  constant — and the loop iterations are where the cache pays most.\n\nNet effect: within the 5-minute TTL, loop iterations and follow-up messages\nread the prompt at ~10% price, and frequent memory writes re-cache ~240\ntokens instead of the ~1000+ of the whole prompt.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: an app calling the Claude API directly with the\n[Anthropic SDK](https://docs.claude.com/en/api/client-sdks) and using\n[prompt caching](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching);\na system prompt with a distinguishable invariant part (instructions) and a\nvolatile part (per-user / per-day data, agent memory). Works for any\nprovider with prefix-based caching — the marker syntax below is\nAnthropic-specific.\n\nYour agent will need from you: your current system-prompt assembly code, and\na list of which parts change per user, per day, or per memory write.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Split `system` into an array of two text blocks, each with\n   `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}`: block one = instructions,\n   guaranteed byte-stable (no interpolated dates, names, or counters);\n   block two = everything volatile (date, profile, memory), ordered\n   rarest-changing first for readability — after the marker it's one hash\n   anyway.\n2. Keep everything *above* the system prompt in the request — the tools\n   array most of all — byte-stable across calls; if you must vary tools per\n   message, make the default selection cover the common case, and never\n   reorder or reword tool schemas casually.\n3. Resist per-message personalization inside cached blocks (top-N memory\n   selection, \"relevant context only\") — selection defeats the cache;\n   include-everything usually costs less.\n4. Read `usage` in every response: `cache_creation_input_tokens` and\n   `cache_read_input_tokens` tell you exactly what happened.\n\nVerify: send two messages within 5 minutes — the second must show\n`cache_read_input_tokens` covering roughly the whole prompt. Then trigger a\nmemory write and send a third message: `cache_read` must still cover the\nstatic block, and `cache_creation` must be roughly the size of your volatile\ntail only. If a memory write causes `cache_creation` the size of the full\nprompt, your static block isn't byte-stable — hunt the interpolated value.\n","numLines":92,"startLine":1,"totalLines":92}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RHnG2WRjWbY2NTSU5KVust","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
